<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finnish People's Delegation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Finnish People's Delegation was the revolutionary government of the Red side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a>, formed by the Social Democrats when the Red Guards seized Helsinki on 28 January 1918. Chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullervo-manner">Kullervo Manner</a>, it ruled the industrial south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and proclaimed the Finnish Socialist Workers' Republic. After the defeats of April 1918 it withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a> and then fled to Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finnish-peoples-delegation.jpg" alt="Finnish People's Delegation" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suomen kansanvaltuuskunta · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suomen_kansanvaltuuskunta_seal_1918.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The decision to launch an armed revolution was taken on 23 January 1918 by the Red Guards' leadership and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s workers' executive committee, a body split from the party committee of the Social Democratic Party of Finland and drawn from the labour movement's most radical wing. On the night of 27 January the committee ordered the arrest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pehr-evind-svinhufvud">Pehr Evind Svinhufvud</a>'s Senate and other leading bourgeois politicians, including 33 members of Parliament, but a premature public handout warned them and they hid. The Delegation occupied the Senate House in Helsinki on 28 January, and the newspaper Työmies announced its members and aims the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its members held roles like ministers: Yrjö Sirola for foreign affairs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-wille-kuusinen">Otto Wille Kuusinen</a> for education, Jalo Kohonen and later Edvard Gylling for finance, and Oskari Tokoi for supply, among others. It issued 45 statutes in all, about two thirds of them answers to urgent administrative problems and the rest aimed at ideological goals or at winning support, drawing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> of 1871, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> and above all the earlier programmes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s labour movement. It also drafted a constitution influenced by the American and Swiss constitutions, on which a referendum was planned.</p><p>Its first act was to close the capitalist newspapers, and on 2 February the counterrevolutionary press was suspended indefinitely, even the right-wing social democratic Työn Valta; the Whites' Senate closed the social democratic papers in turn. The same day it placed the Red Guards under its authority, but it could barely control them, and many guardsmen saw the delegates as parasites far from the front. Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was the only state to recognise the Delegation as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From March, defeats pushed the Reds southeast towards the Karelian Isthmus, and in April the Delegation moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>, near the Russian border. With the Whites about to attack, almost all its members fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on 23 April, which effectively dissolved it; Oskari Tokoi went to the United Kingdom and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The Red Guards left to defend Viipuri branded them traitors. Excluded from the amnesty of late 1918, the delegates lived in exile and led the underground Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from Russia, and in the late 1930s many survivors were executed in the Great Purge.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Finnish People's Delegation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 28 January the Red Guards seized Helsinki, and the <b>Finnish People's Delegation</b>, chaired by Kullervo Manner, took power in the industrial south.</blockquote><a href="/state/finnish-socialist-workers-republic">Finnish Socialist Workers' Republic</a></li></ul></section>
